on every computer he ever uses?  Including ones
where he isn't the system administrator and does
not have the ability or permission to install software?

(Not to mention, Thunderbird is the second-worst
newsreader in the history of the known universe.)

There is of course the portable-install option
(using e.g. a USB Flash drive), but that has its
own associated problems, not least the need
to manage separate binaries for various OSes.
(AFAIK, the only filesystem in existence on
which you can install binaries for all the major
OSes and expect to be able to run them and
not have a lot of attribute-related headaches
is ISO9660, which is read-only.  FAT32 can
be read and written everywhere but doesn't
support file ownership or permissions to a
level that will make Unix-like OSes happy;
it's fine for data files, but software installs
on FAT32 are problematic at best.  And if
you partition your USB mass storage
device and create multiple filesystems for
different OSes, Windows throws up its
hands and doesn't know what to do.)


